{
  // A positive allowlist, not an exclude list, and that is the whole design.
  //
  // What this gates: dependency hygiene. A declared dependency nothing imports
  // is supply-chain surface nobody is watching; an *unlisted* one is worse,
  // because the build resolves it today through somebody else's transitive tree
  // and stops the day they tidy their own.
  //
  // What it does not gate, and why an `exclude` list was the wrong tool for
  // saying so. knip enables every issue type it knows except three by default,
  // so `exclude` describes the gate only for the categories that existed when
  // it was written — a knip minor that adds a default-on category silently
  // widens a blocking CI job. It also hid two mistakes on the first attempt
  // here: `nsExports`/`nsTypes` were listed and are knip's own defaults
  // (no-ops), while `files`, `duplicates` and `namespaceMembers` stayed gated
  // despite being exactly what the reasoning below says must not be.
  //
  // `files` in particular would be a trap: no `entry`/`project` is declared, so
  // the worker/ tree is reachable only because knip parses the `worker` script
  // in package.json, and scripts/ci/ only via the github-actions plugin. Change
  // either invocation shape and CI reports the worker as dead code. Worse, the
  // ordinary case of landing a module in one commit and importing it in the
  // next would fail a check whose message talks about dead files.
  //
  // The unused-*export* categories are the ones this repo cannot gate at all.
  // Run bare, knip reports ~110 of them, and that is not a backlog — it
  // conflates three things:
  //
  //   - genuinely dead code (three functions, deleted in the commit that added
  //     this file);
  //   - functions used inside their own module, where the fix is dropping the
  //     `export` keyword rather than the function. `hoursForTicket` is declared
  //     at lib/sla/index.ts:69 and called four times from the same file, at
  //     lines 133, 196, 240 and 315 — the furthest 246 lines below it, so a
  //     reader who checks only the neighbourhood of the declaration will
  //     wrongly conclude it is dead;
  //   - exports whose only consumer is not an import at all. `handlers` and
  //     `PLANNED_JOB_TYPES` in worker/handlers/index.ts are read by
  //     scripts/ci/repo-rules.mjs with a regex, so deleting them on knip's word
  //     would break the job-registry check that keeps the three job registries
  //     agreeing.
  //
  // Gating *this tool's answer* would need a ~110-line ignore list — the
  // "exemption list" AGENTS.md says not to add your call site to — or a
  // sweeping deletion whose third case is actively wrong. So knip's own export
  // categories stay a hand-run: `npm run knip:exports` covers exactly the
  // complement of the list below.
  //
  // **The question itself is gated, though, by scripts/ci/repo-rules.mjs.** Its
  // `dead-exports` check landed separately and answers the same question
  // properly: it walks the import graph rather than counting identifiers, so an
  // export is live only when another module names it in an `import` or a
  // re-export, and it knows the framework conventions (`GET`, `metadata`,
  // `dynamic`) that produce most of knip's ~110. That is why the three cases
  // above are a reason to distrust *knip's* number rather than a reason the
  // repo cannot have the check — an earlier version of this comment said the
  // latter, and it was wrong the day the other check merged.
  //
  // What repo-rules deliberately does not cover is an exported *type*, so that
  // half is the real content of the hand-run.
  //
  // "Exactly" is checked rather than asserted, and it did not hold when this
  // was first written: knip 6.35.1 knows seventeen issue types, six are gated
  // here and the hand-run named eight, so `catalog`, `catalogReferences` and
  // `cycles` were covered by neither — which is the same shape of hole an
  // `exclude` list would have left. Two of the three cannot fire on this repo
  // (npm has no catalog protocol), and that is exactly why nobody would have
  // noticed the sentence was false; `cycles` can.
  "include": [
    "dependencies",
    "devDependencies",
    "optionalPeerDependencies",
    "unlisted",
    "unresolved",
    "binaries",
  ],
}
